siteconf and its Perl libraries:

    brew install rapidcow/fresh/siteconf

The libraries are available separately:

    brew install rapidcow/fresh/codesets
    brew install rapidcow/fresh/textdomain
    brew install rapidcow/fresh/gengenlib
    brew install rapidcow/fresh/genlib

gengenlib includes template-provider and Template Toolkit.  genlib
includes URI.  siteconf installs these libraries as dependencies,
along with its own Perl modules, templates, command, and manual.
The modules use Homebrew Perl's vendor directories, so Homebrew
Perl can load them without setting PERL5LIB.  Use Homebrew Perl
when running configure and render scripts generated by siteconf.

Translations are optional and can be installed independently:

    brew install rapidcow/fresh/siteconf-l10n-en
    brew install rapidcow/fresh/siteconf-l10n-zh

Each language pulls in siteconf-l10n, the shared Perl module that
locates the catalogs under Homebrew's share/locale directory.
The language packages can coexist and do not require the CLI.

Each formula selects its package's version tag.  The downloader
fetches that tag and creates a SHA-256-checked archive of just the
package subtree.  It fixes the archive timestamps to the Unix
epoch, so moving a tag to another commit with the same subtree
keeps the checksum unchanged.  Changed package content fails
verification, even when its tag name stays the same.

To reproduce a checksum in the siteconf repository, for example:

    git -c tar.umask=0002 \
        -c 'tar.tar.gz.command=git archive gzip' \
        archive --format=tar.gz --mtime=1970-01-01T00:00:00Z \
        codesets/v0.00_00:codesets | shasum -a 256

Only the latest package versions are maintained.  Update each
package's tag, version, and checksum together; template-provider
has its own tag and checksum in the gengenlib formula.  When a tag
moves with changed content at the same version, update its checksum
and bump the formula revision.  Use brew fetch --force to discard
an old cached archive when checking a moved tag.

Use brew upgrade to keep installed packages current.  After a
Homebrew Perl major/minor upgrade, reinstall these packages for
its new vendor directory.

